### Short bio

I spent fifteen years running operations in building services, finishing as Operations Director at Harlow & Pike where I grew the HVAC division from £4.2m to £11.8m. I fixed the same problems there that I now see in almost every M&E and electrical contracting business I talk to: margin that disappears by the time the job closes, an MD who's still answering every site call, and retentions sitting unpaid for months. I work with eight building services contractors at a time, getting into the numbers and the operation from day one.

### One-line introduction

Zander Demo is a business coach for M&E and HVAC contractors, with a background growing a building services division from £4.2m to £11.8m, helping MDs fix margin bleed, get out of the weeds, and stop retentions sitting idle.

### For partners and events

Zander Demo spent fifteen years in building services, finishing as Operations Director at Harlow & Pike where he grew the HVAC division from £4.2m to £11.8m over five years. He achieved that by moving the business from one-off installs to planned maintenance contracts and tightening job pricing to protect margin through growth. He now coaches MDs in M&E, HVAC and electrical contracting businesses, working with up to eight clients at a time on margin, owner dependency, and cash flow. He is based in Leeds and works with contractors across the UK.

===== market:options (26s, 3285 chars)
Zander's strongest market is HVAC and M&E contractors, where his Harlow & Pike proof lands immediately and the three problems he solves are universal.

| Option | Value | Pain | Growing | Easy to find | Purchasing power |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| HVAC and M&E contractors (£2m–£20m) | 5 | 5 | 4 | 5 | 4 |
| Electrical contractors (£1m–£15m) | 4 | 5 | 4 | 4 | 4 |
| Facilities management firms (own in-house maintenance) | 3 | 4 | 4 | 3 | 3 |

### HVAC and M&E contractors

This is where Zander's proof is sharpest. Growing a HVAC division from £4.2m to £11.8m by moving from one-off installs to planned maintenance contracts is exactly the kind of story an MD in this space respects, because it describes their own business and what it could become. Margin bleed, retentions sitting unpaid, and an owner buried in site calls are not occasional problems here - they are the standard operating condition. MDs in HVAC and M&E are straightforward to find on LinkedIn, they sign off all buying decisions themselves, and at £2m to £20m turnover they carry the purchasing power to pay £1,950 a month without the fee becoming the conversation.

### Electrical contractors

Electrical contractors share the same pain profile as HVAC and M&E: jobs that look profitable at the quote stage and bleed margin by completion, owners who are the hub of every decision, and cash tied up in retentions. Zander's operational background transfers cleanly - the job costing disciplines and the shift to recurring revenue are as relevant here as in HVAC. The sector scores slightly lower on value because Zander's named proof sits in HVAC, not electrical, so there is a small credibility gap to bridge in the first conversation. That said, the problems and the buyer type are near-identical, making this a strong secondary market.

### Facilities management firms

FM firms running their own in-house maintenance teams face similar operational pressure, but the profile is less consistent. Larger FM firms are often divisions of bigger groups where the MD does not hold the buying decision. Smaller owner-led FM businesses exist but are harder to identify cleanly on LinkedIn, and purchasing power varies more widely. Zander's proof applies, but the connection requires more explanation than it does with HVAC or electrical. This market is worth keeping in view, but it is not the place to start.

### Recommended core client

> My core client is an MD or owner of an HVAC, M&E or mechanical and electrical contracting business with 10 to 50 employees doing £1M to £20M revenue.

> **If HVAC and electrical feel equally close on the Decision Call:** Ask the prospect one question: how does your business make most of its money - installs, planned maintenance, or a mix? If the answer involves planned maintenance contracts or service agreements, you are in HVAC and M&E territory and your Harlow & Pike story lands directly. If it is project-by-project electrical installation with no recurring element, you are in electrical contracting and you lead with the job costing and margin discipline story instead. Both are strong. Pick the market where you have spoken to at least three owners already, because early conversations build the pattern recognition that makes the next ten calls easier.

===== market:avatar (48s, 6396 chars)
Margin-Blind Mike: MD of a 25-person M&E contracting firm in the North of England, winning more work than ever and wondering where the money keeps going.

- **Name:** Mike
- **Age:** 42 to 54
- **Location:** North of England, Midlands, or Scotland - typically within two hours of a major conurbation
- **Business:** M&E or HVAC contracting firm, mix of installs and some service work
- **Revenue:** £2m to £12m
- **Team:** 15 to 40 staff - engineers, site supervisors, an admin or two, maybe a contracts manager
- **Role:** MD or Owner. Also still the de facto estimator, site escalation point, and main client contact
- **Time in the business:** 10 to 25 years - built it himself or took over from a parent

### A day in the life

It is 7.15am on a Tuesday. Mike is in the van on the A1, heading to a site in Doncaster before the office opens, because the main contractor called him at 6.50 to say one of his engineers didn't show and they want answers from the top. He had planned to spend this morning reviewing the pricing on a £380k tender that needs to go out by Friday. That won't happen now. His phone buzzes twice before he hits the dual carriageway: one message from his contracts manager asking whether to approve a materials order, one from a customer chasing a service report that should have gone out last week. He answers both while stationary at a junction. By the time he walks onto site he has a loose plan to get back to the office by eleven, review the tender over lunch, and spend the afternoon on a job review for a contract that closed six weeks ago but whose final account still doesn't stack up. He knows the job lost money. He isn't sure how much, or exactly where it went. That is what he keeps meaning to look at properly.

### In his words

> We're busier than we've ever been. The order book's full, the lads are flat out, we're winning decent work. So why does it feel like I'm running faster just to stay in the same place? Last year we turned over more than the year before and I took less home. I don't fully understand that. I know some jobs ran over, I know there were a few retention holdbacks that dragged on, and I know we had to price tight on a couple of contracts to keep the guys busy through winter. But I can't put my finger on exactly where it went. My accountant gives me the year-end numbers and I nod along, but by then it's history. I spend my days firefighting and my evenings wondering if I'm actually building something or just creating a job for myself that I can never leave. I keep thinking if I could just get on top of things, if I could get the right people doing the right stuff, it would feel different. I'm not sure what different looks like though, if I'm honest.

### What they want

- To know, in real time, whether a job is making or losing money - before it's too late to do anything about it.
- To stop being the person every problem escalates to. To trust the contracts manager, the supervisor, the office to handle it.
- To grow revenue without the wheels coming off - more work, same margin, not more chaos.
- To take a proper holiday without the phone ringing every other day.
- To build something they can eventually step back from, sell, or hand on - not a business that only works because they're in it.

### What they fear

- Winning a big contract, throwing resource at it, and finding out twelve months later the job lost £80k.
- A key engineer or supervisor walking out and taking half the site knowledge with them.
- Cash running dry in a bad month because three retentions are still sitting unpaid and a VAT bill just landed.
- Handing over responsibility to someone in the team and it going wrong in a way that costs the business its reputation with a main contractor.
- Working this hard in ten years' time and having nothing to show for it structurally - no equity, no exit, just more of the same.

### What they have tried

- Hired a contracts manager to take the pressure off, but still ends up pulled in because the CM doesn't have the authority or the confidence to make calls.
- Bought estimating or job costing software that the team uses inconsistently, so the numbers are never quite right.
- Had conversations with their accountant about profitability, got a set of reports they don't fully trust, and went back to running on instinct.
- Taken on a business adviser through a local growth scheme who gave good general advice but had never run a site in their life.

### What they suspect

- That coaches and consultants charge good money to tell you things you already know, dressed up in a framework.
- That anyone who hasn't worked in building services or run a live contract will waste the first six months just learning how the industry works.
- That most of the advice they'd get is generic - fix your systems, delegate more, set KPIs - without any understanding of retentions, subbies, variation orders, or how a main contractor actually behaves.
- That they could probably figure it out themselves if they had more time, which they never do.

### What finally makes them act

- A job closes and the final account comes in £60k under where it should have been, with no clear explanation.
- They miss a child's school event, a weekend away, or a conversation with their partner - and feel it.
- A retention that's been sitting unpaid for fourteen months gets written off, and they realise nobody was chasing it.
- A trusted engineer hands in his notice and they suddenly see how much of the operation lives in one person's head.
- They do the maths on what they took home last year against the hours they worked, and the number shocks them.

### How they talk

| Thing | What they call it |
| --- | --- |
| Customers | Clients, main contractors, or the client - rarely 'customers' |
| Staff | The lads, the engineers, the guys on the tools, the office |
| Jobs | Contracts, projects, schemes, or jobs |
| Money owed | Retentions, holdbacks, final account, Applications for Payment |
| Profit on a job | Margin - or what's left when the dust settles |
| Subcontractors | Subbies |
| Changes to scope | Variations, extras, or change orders |

### Ready to act

> I've built a decent business but I'm not sure it's working for me anymore. I need to understand where the margin's going, get the right people making decisions without me, and actually start running this place instead of being run by it.

