- **8** Maximum clients carried at one time, so each gets proper attention
- **15+ years** Inside building services, from project engineer to operations director

### Who I help

My core client is the MD or owner of an HVAC, M&E or electrical contracting business turning over £1m to £20m with 10 to 50 staff. They built the business themselves, or inherited it and grew it. They are the best estimator in the room, the first call when a site goes wrong, and the person signing off decisions that their contracts manager or supervisors should be handling. The order book is full. The margins are not. They are working harder than ever and quietly wondering whether any of it is actually building something, or whether they are just creating a job they can never leave.

### The problems I fix

- Margin that disappears by the time the job closes, with no clear picture of where it went until it is too late to do anything about it.
- The MD stuck answering every site call and signing off every order, because the team doesn't have the authority or the confidence to make decisions without them.
- Retentions sitting unpaid for months with nobody chasing them, quietly draining cash while the next job ramps up.
- Revenue growing but profit not following, and an owner who can't explain the gap to themselves, let alone their accountant.

### How I work

1. **First conversation.** A straight conversation about the business: where the margin is going, where the owner is spending their time, and what the next twelve months need to look like. No pitch, no framework presentation. I want to understand the operation before I say anything useful.
2. **Into the numbers and the operation.** From day one I get into job reviews, pricing discussions and the weekly numbers. I bring a rhythm of visibility that most building services businesses don't have, so owners can see problems forming before they become expensive. This feels more like hands-on consulting early on, and that is deliberate.
3. **Building the business that works without you.** Over time we shift decisions down to the right people, tighten the model so margin holds through growth, and build the structure that lets the owner run the business instead of being run by it. Sessions run Tuesdays and Wednesdays. Progress is measured in numbers, not conversations.

### Results

- Grew the HVAC division at Harlow & Pike from £4.2m to £11.8m in five years by shifting from one-off installs to planned maintenance contracts and tightening job pricing to protect margin through growth.
- Fixed the same margin, dependency and retention problems at Harlow & Pike that I now see in almost every M&E and electrical contracting business I talk to, from the inside, running a live operation.

### Working together

If you work with MDs or owners of building services businesses, the clients most likely to benefit from an introduction are the ones who are growing but frustrated: revenue is up, the order book is healthy, but profit is thinner than it should be and the owner is still the hub of every decision. Accountants often see it first in the year-end numbers. Solicitors see it when an owner starts thinking about exit and realises the business doesn't run without them. Finance brokers see it when a cash flow problem turns out to be a retentions problem nobody has been managing. An introduction to Zander costs nothing and gives your client access to someone who has run a building services operation at this scale, not someone who has read about it. The minimum engagement is £1,950 a month. Zander carries a maximum of eight clients, so capacity is limited.

- **LinkedIn:** linkedin.com/in/zander-demo
- **Web:** Profit Coach page (profitcoach.co.uk/zander-demo)
- **Location:** Leeds, West Yorkshire. Works with contractors across the UK.

