===== offer:direction (45s, 5982 chars)
Zander helps MDs in M&E, HVAC and electrical contracting firms get real visibility on job margin and get out of the weeds, without spending months explaining how the industry works, using a weekly numbers rhythm built on fifteen years of running live operations.

### The gap

| Today | What they want |
| --- | --- |
| Turnover rising, take-home flat. Jobs close and the final account is short, but nobody can say exactly where the margin went. | Real-time visibility on job margin so problems are caught at week four, not month twelve when the damage is done. |
| Retentions sitting unpaid for months with nobody accountable for chasing them. In a tight month, that gap is the difference between comfortable and scrambling. | A retention tracker with a named person chasing every holdback on a set schedule, and cash moving again. |
| MD is the escalation point for every site call, every materials sign-off, every client complaint. The contracts manager is good but still rings for decisions he should own. | A team that makes calls without the MD, with the owner stepping in only when it genuinely needs them. |
| No proper holiday in years. Four days off last summer and the phone never stopped. The business only works because the MD is always in it. | A week away without the phone ringing. A business that runs without the owner in the room, and eventually something worth selling or stepping back from. |

### Why they will buy

- Know-how: Mike has been burned before by advisers who gave him generic frameworks and had never set foot on a live site. Zander ran the operation himself, knows what a retention looks like, how a variation gets priced, and what happens when a main contractor squeezes the programme. There is no learning period.
- Speed: Most MDs in this position have been saying they'll sort it themselves for two or three years. The time to fix it keeps getting consumed by the same problems it would fix. Zander gets into job reviews and pricing discussions from week one and gets to the answer faster.
- Ease and less stress: The work targets the hours and pressure Mike wastes on decisions others should be making. The two or three hours a day reclaimed by shifting calls down to the right people come from the coaching, not on top of it.
- Fit: Zander only works with eight contractors at a time. Mike is not joining a programme full of retailers and consultants. Every conversation is grounded in contracts, subbies, retentions and site reality.

### Key messages

1. Winning more work than ever and taking less home is not a growth problem. It is a margin problem. More work through a leaky system produces more stress, not more profit. The fix starts with knowing, in real time, which jobs are making money and which are bleeding it.
2. Retentions do not chase themselves. Tens of thousands sitting idle in holdbacks is not bad luck, it is a missing process. One person, one tracker, one schedule changes it, and in most businesses that money is already earned.
3. The MD being the bottleneck for every decision is not a sign the team is weak. It is a sign the structure has not caught up with the size of the business. Shifting decisions to the right level is the single fastest way to get hours back.
4. A job that closed six weeks ago with a final account that does not stack up is not a mystery to be shelved. It is a pattern that will repeat on every similar scheme until someone gets into the numbers and finds where the margin went.
5. A business that only works because the MD is always in it is not a business yet. It is a job that the owner cannot leave. Building something that runs, and eventually something worth selling or stepping back from, starts with getting out of the operational middle.

### The offer

- **Name:** The Building Services Growth Programme
- **Promise:** Get proper visibility on where the margin is going, get the MD out of the operational middle, and get unpaid retentions moving again, with an operator alongside you who has already fixed exactly these problems in a building services business.
- **Who it is for:** MDs and owners of M&E, HVAC and electrical contracting firms turning over £2m to £12m, with 15 to 40 staff, who are winning work but can't explain why profit is not following.
- **What is included:** Weekly sessions for the first four to six weeks to get into the numbers fast and land early wins on margin and cash. Then two 90-minute sessions a month, ongoing. Zander gets into job reviews and pricing discussions from day one. Between sessions: direct access for anything that can't wait. BOSS Scorecard completed in the first week to show exactly which area of the business is the current ceiling.
- **Format:** Hybrid. Calls on Tuesdays and Wednesdays, 08:00 to 14:00. Site or office visits where they add value, particularly early on. Maximum eight clients at any one time.
- **Price options:** £1,950 per month, rolling. Six months upfront available at a reduced rate - ask on the first call. Fee rises with each new client intake. One recovered retention or one job that does not bleed margin typically covers the first three months.

### Why you

- Grew the HVAC division at Harlow & Pike from £4.2m to £11.8m in five years, by shifting from one-off installs to planned maintenance contracts and tightening job pricing to hold margin through growth.
- Fifteen years inside building services: Project Engineer, Contracts Manager, then Operations Director. Has lived through margin bleed, owner dependency and retention cash problems, not read about them.
- Gets into job reviews and pricing discussions from week one. Clients do not spend months explaining how the industry works.
- Carries a maximum of eight clients. Each one gets proper time, not a slot on a conveyor belt.
- Straight-talking and specific. The problems Zander fixes are things the owner usually suspects but cannot pin down precisely. The work makes them visible in numbers, then builds a rhythm to keep them visible.

===== offer:payment (39s, 4833 chars)
The fee is never the number. It is what the number buys compared to what margin bleed, idle retentions, and another year in the weeds are already costing the business.

| Option | Investment | Best for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Monthly, rolling | £1,950 per month | MDs who want to start without a long commitment and prove the value in the first three months. |
| Six months upfront | £10,800 (saving £900 against monthly) | MDs who are ready to commit and want to move quickly. Full payment or 50% upfront then the balance at month three. |
| Six months upfront, split three ways | £3,600 now, £3,600 at month two, £3,600 at month four | Owners who prefer to spread the outlay but still want the upfront rate. |

### If they ask the price early

**Anchor script - price asked before the call**

```text
Aye, fair question. The MDs I work with invest anywhere up to £50,000 a year with a coach, but you can start with me for under £2,000 a month. Where it lands for you depends on what's actually going on in the business - how many jobs, what the margin picture looks like, how much of your time's getting eaten by decisions that shouldn't need you. That's worth fifteen minutes to work out properly. I've got Monday or Thursday afternoon free this week - which suits you better?
```

_Use this when a prospect asks the fee before you have had a proper conversation. It anchors high, names the entry point, and moves straight to booking the call. Do not quote a specific package until you are on the call._

### At the end of the call

**Either-or close - end of discovery call**

```text
Right, so based on what you've told me - the margin coming in short on that Sheffield job, retentions that haven't moved in months, and you still fielding calls you shouldn't be near - this is exactly the kind of business I work with. I've got room for one more client this intake. We can do this monthly at £1,950, or if you want to move fast and lock in six months, it comes down to £10,800 with a couple of ways to split the payments. Which of those fits better with how you like to do things?
```

_Give them both options and stop talking. Do not fill the silence. If they ask for time, agree a specific follow-up day and time before the call ends._

### Proposal outline

1. **Where you are now.** One short paragraph restating what Mike described on the call, in his words. Turnover up, margin short, retentions sitting idle, MD still in the middle of everything. This section proves you listened. If a number came up on the call - a job that came in £60k short, a retention sitting for fourteen months - name it here.
2. **What that is costing.** Make the cost of doing nothing specific and visible. Margin bleed across three or four jobs a year. Retention cash tied up and not working. Two to three hours a day on decisions that should sit elsewhere. Use numbers from the conversation wherever possible.
3. **What changes.** The Building Services Growth Programme in plain terms. Weekly sessions for the first four to six weeks to get into the job numbers fast and land early wins on margin and cash. Then two 90-minute sessions a month, ongoing. Direct access between sessions. BOSS Scorecard in week one to show exactly where the ceiling is. Get into job reviews and pricing discussions from day one.
4. **Why this works with me.** One short paragraph of proof. Grew the HVAC division at Harlow & Pike from £4.2m to £11.8m in five years by fixing the same problems: tightened job pricing, shifted to planned maintenance contracts, held margin through growth. Fifteen years inside building services. No learning period. Maximum eight clients.
5. **Your investment.** State both options clearly. Monthly at £1,950, or six months upfront at £10,800 with payment split options. Add one honest line: one recovered retention or one job that doesn't bleed margin typically covers the first three months.
6. **Next step.** One sentence. 'If this looks right, reply to this email and I will send the agreement over. We can start the first session next Tuesday or Wednesday.'

### Raising your price

Start at £1,950 a month and raise by £500 for every two new clients you bring in. At four clients, the monthly goes to £2,450 for anyone joining after that point. At six clients, £2,950. Existing clients stay on the rate they started at. You never go back and reprice someone mid-engagement. By the time you reach eight clients, your blended monthly rate will be well above your starting point and your capacity will be full. At that point you have a waiting list and you set the rate for the next intake at whatever the market supports. If a prospect pushes back on the fee, the answer is not a discount. It is a clear conversation about what unresolved margin bleed is already costing them each month, and whether they want to fix it or not.

