### The first issue

**Issue 1**

```text
Subject: That job you still can't explain

You closed a contract six or eight weeks ago. The lads did a good job. The main contractor signed it off. And now the final account has come in short, and nobody can put their finger on exactly where it went.

I've heard this more times than I can count. I've lived it too, early on at Harlow & Pike before we built a rhythm that caught the problems while there was still time to do something about them.

Here is the uncomfortable truth: by the time the final account tells you a job lost money, it's already history. The variation you could have raised at week five. The materials overrun that crept past the budget in week eight. The day-rate labour that ran three weeks longer than the programme allowed. All of it was visible while it was happening. Nobody was looking at the right numbers often enough to see it.

This is not about better software. It is about a weekly rhythm.

At Harlow & Pike, the single biggest shift we made was moving from monthly job reviews to a weekly numbers check on every live contract. Not a long meeting. Thirty minutes, the right four or five figures, the same format every week. Cost to date against budget. Variations raised against variations agreed. Labour hours against the programme. Retention position.

Within a month, the contracts manager stopped ringing me to say a job was in trouble. He started ringing to say he'd spotted something at week four and wanted to raise a variation before it got worse. That is a different conversation entirely.

The job you still can't explain probably had a moment, somewhere around week five or six, where someone on the ground knew something was drifting. They didn't flag it because there was no rhythm that made flagging it normal. And by the time the final account closed, the drift had become a write-off.

The practical action this month is simple. Pick one live contract, ideally one where your gut says the margin might be soft. Sit down with your contracts manager for thirty minutes and look at four things: cost to date, budget to complete, variations raised versus agreed, and labour hours against programme. Don't worry about making it formal. Just look at the numbers together and ask the question: if this job closed today, what would we actually make?

That conversation, on a regular basis, is where margin starts getting protected.

If you want to talk through where the margin is going in your business and what to fix first, I offer a free 45-minute session with no agenda other than getting specific. No frameworks, no slide decks. Just a straight conversation about your numbers.

Book it at [Profit Coach page URL] or reply to this email and we'll find a time.

Zander Demo
Building Services Margin Letter
```

_Send on the first Monday of the month. Distribute via LinkedIn newsletter and email. Keep the tone exactly as written: plain, specific, grounded in site reality._

